const reserve = `BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
WITH taken AS (
  UPDATE places
  SET remaining = remaining - 1
  WHERE place_id = 21 AND remaining > 0
  RETURNING place_id
)
INSERT INTO reservations (request_id, place_id, owner_id)
SELECT 'R1', place_id, 7 FROM taken
RETURNING request_id;
COMMIT;`;
const invariant = `SELECT p.place_id, p.capacity, p.remaining,
       count(r.request_id) AS reserved,
       p.remaining + count(r.request_id) = p.capacity AS agrees
FROM places AS p
LEFT JOIN reservations AS r USING (place_id)
GROUP BY p.place_id
ORDER BY p.place_id;`;
---

<p>A transaction makes a group of database changes succeed or fail together. That is enough to stop a failed reservation consuming a place. It does not, by itself, stop two requests both deciding that the last place is theirs.</p>
<p>Correct concurrent work needs a second argument: what prevents another writer changing the facts between our decision and its effect? Sometimes the answer is one conditional statement. Sometimes a database constraint settles the question. A rule spanning several rows may need a locking protocol or serialisable transactions, together with a way to repeat an aborted attempt.</p>
<p>We will reserve places in a class, following one rule from a broken implementation to a complete working change. The SQL uses <strong>PostgreSQL 18</strong>; selected concurrent schedules were run on native PostgreSQL 18.6. The figures are labelled teaching models, separate from those observations. Finish this first case before choosing the deeper articles on lock design or isolation.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="start-with-the-fact-that-must-remain-true">Start with the fact that must remain true</h2>
<p>Class 21 has one place. Mina and Tariq each want it. The service stores a row for the class in <code>places</code>, including its capacity and remaining places, and one row in <code>reservations</code> for each accepted booking. To keep this exercise small, reservations have no cancelled status: cancelling removes the row.</p>
<p>The rule that must survive every committed change is an <strong>invariant</strong>. Ours has three parts:</p>
<ul>
 <li>For each class, remaining places plus reservation rows equals capacity, and remaining places is never negative.</li>
 <li>Each reservation names an existing class and an owner. An owner can have at most one reservation in that class.</li>
 <li>A successful request records its reservation and consumes exactly one place. A rejected request does neither.</li>
</ul>
<p>Either person may get the last place. Accepting both is forbidden. Rejecting both because of a temporary failure is recoverable, provided the application reports the failure honestly. That distinction gives us something precise to check beyond “the statements returned no error”.</p>
<p>The public booking form is one writer. Cancellations, moves between classes, capacity changes, imports and staff corrections can change the same rule. Write them down. Protecting the booking endpoint alone cannot establish an invariant that an import can bypass.</p>

<h3 id="put-the-complete-change-inside-a-boundary">Put the complete change inside a boundary</h3>
<p>Decrementing the counter and recording ownership belong in one transaction. If the insert fails, the decrement must go too. <code>BEGIN</code> starts an explicit transaction; <code>COMMIT</code> asks to keep its changes; <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards them. PostgreSQL also runs an individual statement in an implicit transaction when there is no explicit block. Its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-transactions.html">transaction tutorial</a> defines these boundaries.</p>
<p>While Mina’s transaction is open, it can see its own decrement and reservation. An ordinary query from another PostgreSQL session cannot see those uncommitted changes. After commit they belong to the committed state together; when another reader sees them depends on that reader’s isolation and snapshot. A reader already using an older snapshot need not change its view at the commit.</p>
<p>In the native check, an observer still read one remaining place after the first transaction decremented it but before commit. Afterwards it read zero and one reservation. A second test deliberately inserted a duplicate request identity after a decrement. The insert failed, and rolling the transaction back restored the counter. This checks atomicity in those cases. It says nothing yet about two decisions racing.</p>
<p>A savepoint marks a point within a transaction to which selected work can be rolled back. Earlier work remains pending. That is useful only if the remaining transaction still makes sense: retaining the decrement while discarding its reservation would break our rule. Keep transactions short enough to finish their protected work promptly, and leave unrelated computation and external calls outside their lifetime.</p>

<h3 id="let-the-database-reject-invalid-states-where-it-can">Let the database reject invalid states where it can</h3>
<p>The exercise schema uses a primary key for each request, uniqueness for the class–owner pair, a foreign key for the class reference, and required values and checks for valid counts. Those rules apply to every ordinary writer subject to the constraints, including concurrent writers. An application query asking “does R1 exist?” does not reserve that identity; another request can insert it before ours does.</p>
<p>A check that <code>remaining BETWEEN 0 AND capacity</code> rejects an invalid row. It does not compare that row with all the reservation rows. PostgreSQL <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html#DDL-CONSTRAINTS-CHECK-CONSTRAINTS">does not support CHECK constraints as enforcement over other rows</a>. Our equality between counter and reservations still needs a change protocol. Nor can a constraint prove that an external payment was taken or that an authenticated caller owns the supplied owner number.</p>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/#make-invalid-facts-difficult-to-store">Designing relational data</a> develops the representation and constraint choices. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> handles adding enforcement while older data and writers still exist.</p>

<h2 id="two-correct-looking-transactions-can-make-an-incorrect-result">Two correct-looking transactions can make an incorrect result</h2>
<p>Consider an application that reads <code>remaining = 1</code>, calculates <code>1 − 1 = 0</code>, then sends <code>UPDATE places SET remaining = 0</code> and inserts its reservation. Each request uses a transaction. Run either alone and the rule holds.</p>
<p>Step through Figure 1 with <em>Read, then write zero</em>. The state counter shows committed rows, so it changes at commit. Watch which observation Tariq’s transaction uses for its write. At the last step, switch to <em>Conditional decrement</em> to compare the outcomes, then reset and follow where it waits.</p>
<ReservationRace />
<p>The broken version finishes with zero remaining places and two reservation rows for capacity one. Each transaction committed all its writes, and neither stored a negative counter. The application lost a decrement by overwriting the current value with a calculation made earlier. Transaction boundaries and row checks did not protect the decision.</p>
<p>This is a labelled illustrative schedule, not a claim that every engine permits it at every isolation level. The corresponding native PostgreSQL Read Committed run did permit both commits. Its final query returned <code>remaining = 0</code> and <code>reserved = 2</code>.</p>

<h3 id="the-protection-must-cover-the-decision-as-well-as-the-write">The protection must cover the decision as well as the write</h3>
<p>Changing the write to <code>remaining = remaining - 1</code> avoids overwriting a counter with a stale calculation. Adding <code>remaining &gt; 0</code> also asks the database to check the business condition as it changes that row. Those are useful repairs, but neither implies that every decision can be reduced to one row.</p>
<p>For a different rule, suppose Mina and Tariq are the class’s two instructors and at least one must remain available. Each may leave if the other is available. Their transactions read both rows, then update their own row. They never write the same row, yet together they can break the rule.</p>
<p>Step through the two departures in Figure 2. At the final step, switch from <em>Repeatable Read</em> to <em>Serializable</em> and compare the committed availability and B’s next decision.</p>
<StaffingFigure />
<p>A <strong>read dependency</strong> is a fact a transaction relied on: A’s decision needs Tariq’s availability to remain compatible with its change. The query <code>WHERE available</code> also describes a <strong>predicate</strong>, a condition selecting a set. Decisions about a set can be invalidated by new matching rows as well as changes to existing ones. Counting reservations then inserting another is one such case; an empty result does not supply a row to lock.</p>

<h3 id="a-repeatable-view-does-not-by-itself-prove-the-rule">A repeatable view does not by itself prove the rule</h3>
<p>A stable view makes repeated reads agree. It can still let two transactions act on the same old facts. In our native staffing test, both PostgreSQL Repeatable Read transactions counted two available instructors, updated different rows and committed. The final available count was zero.</p>
<p><strong>Serialisable execution</strong> promises that committed transactions have an effect equivalent to some order in which they ran one at a time. In Figure 2, whichever instructor leaves first, the second must see only one available instructor and stay. Accepting both cannot match either order. If each transaction preserves the rule when run alone, and all relevant transactions participate in the serialisable contract, serial equivalence gives us the argument that their committed combination preserves it too.</p>
<p>That guarantee does not choose who wins or make an arbitrary read fresh. Serial equivalence alone also does not establish an ordering contract for real-time observations across clients, replicas or services. A claim such as “a later request must see the earlier acknowledgement” needs the engine and read path’s documented guarantees. The <a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/#a-serial-order-does-not-establish-a-real-time-order">isolation article</a> separates those questions.</p>

<h2 id="choose-protection-for-the-invariant-you-actually-have">Choose protection for the invariant you actually have</h2>
<p>Choose the smallest protection whose argument covers every way to break the rule. For one row’s bounded counter, put the change and condition together. For a forbidden duplicate, let a unique constraint arbitrate. When the decision depends on several rows, identify what coordinates those rows or use a suitable serialisable contract.</p>

<h3 id="express-a-change-and-its-condition-together">Express a change and its condition together</h3>
<p>Here is the complete reservation for the counter design. The <code>taken</code> expression returns a class only if the conditional update changed it. The insert consumes that result, so a full class produces no reservation. This uses PostgreSQL’s data-changing <code>WITH</code> and <code>RETURNING</code> clauses; the application sends a fresh request identity and owner as bound values in real use.</p>
<Sql code={reserve} label="Reserve one place and record ownership together, in PostgreSQL" />
<p>One returned request row followed by successful commit means this attempt reserved a place. Zero returned rows means the class was absent or had no remaining place; this compact statement intentionally treats both as unavailable. A service needing separate responses must distinguish them without weakening its protection. A constraint error is a failed attempt, not a zero-row business result. Roll the failed transaction back.</p>
<p>In the native two-session check at Read Committed, A’s conditional update took the row first. B’s update waited. A inserted its reservation and committed; B then changed zero rows. The observed wait and recheck agree with PostgreSQL’s documented <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html#XACT-READ-COMMITTED">handling of concurrent updates</a>. At another isolation setting B may instead receive an abort; its failure handling must match that contract.</p>
<p>This argument depends on the other writers. Cancellation must remove an existing reservation and restore one place in the same transaction, exactly once. A capacity change must adjust capacity and remaining together without making remaining negative. A move must protect both classes and the ownership change. Staff tools must not set counters independently. Otherwise the counter can drift even though this reservation statement is correct.</p>
<p>For an edit made from an older screen, a version token can express a different condition: update this row only if its version still equals the one the editor read, and advance the version on success. Zero changed rows then signals that the expected version is no longer current, or the row is missing. Every relevant writer must advance the token. A token on one row cannot protect an unrelated count over many rows.</p>

<h3 id="coordinate-decisions-that-span-several-rows">Coordinate decisions that span several rows</h3>
<p>Suppose we remove the remaining counter and calculate free places from reservations. That removes a stored copy that could drift, but now “count, compare, insert” spans rows. One approach gives the decision a stable coordination row: lock class 21 before counting its reservations, then keep that lock until the insert commits or rolls back.</p>
<p>For PostgreSQL Read Committed, participants can first acquire the class row with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code>, then use a separate statement to read the reservation count after acquiring it. That later statement gets the view needed to include a previous holder’s committed changes. Lock acquisition does not universally refresh a transaction’s earlier snapshot. Every writer that can invalidate this decision must coordinate on the same identity before its protected work.</p>
<p>Locking just today’s reservation rows leaves tomorrow’s insert uncovered. Locking nothing when the set is empty protects nothing. Locking an absent class row also cannot authorise creating an unprotected reservation. The stable class identity, its lifetime and the behaviour of every writer are part of the protocol.</p>
<p>The alternative is to express correct read–decide–write logic in serialisable transactions and handle rejection. It can cover changing sets without hand-designing a coordination point, but it still needs all relevant writers covered and each transaction correct in isolation. <a href="/review/relational-next/locking/">Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> constructs and tests the explicit protocol, including empty sets, inserts and acquisition order.</p>

<h2 id="understand-the-isolation-contract-behind-the-choice">Understand the isolation contract behind the choice</h2>
<p>An isolation name is an entrance to a contract. For the engine and version you intend to use, establish when reads choose their view, how conflicting writes are handled, what locks last until, and what successful commit promises. Also check whether a statement is an ordinary read, a locking read or a change: those can behave differently within one transaction.</p>

<h3 id="versions-and-locks-answer-different-questions">Versions and locks answer different questions</h3>
<p><strong>Multiversion concurrency control</strong>, usually shortened to MVCC, keeps versions so readers can see a permitted earlier state while writes proceed. The version visible to a query answers “what may I read?” A write lock answers “which competing change may proceed?” Versioned reads do not remove the need to arbitrate competing writes. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-intro.html">MVCC introduction</a> explains this division.</p>
<p>Return to Figure 1’s conditional schedule at <em>A records ownership</em>. A’s transaction has changed the row to zero. B’s updater is waiting on that change. An ordinary observer can still read the committed one. These are three different observations of one row at one point in the schedule; there is no contradiction.</p>
<p>In a separate native visibility check, A read one, B changed it to zero and committed, then A read again. At Read Committed A’s second statement read zero. At PostgreSQL Repeatable Read it still read one. Own changes are visible in both. A repeated value therefore tells us about the read view; it does not tell us that nobody changed the row.</p>

<h3 id="stronger-isolation-can-reject-a-result-instead-of-waiting">Stronger isolation can reject a result instead of waiting</h3>
<p>Stronger isolation need not make transactions take turns by blocking every conflicting action. PostgreSQL Serializable tracks dependencies that could make committed work inconsistent with a serial order. Its predicate locks support that detection; they are not ordinary locks that make all relevant writers wait.</p>
<p>In our native Serializable staffing run, both reads returned two and both individual updates succeeded. A committed. B’s <code>COMMIT</code> failed with SQLSTATE <code>40001</code>, a serialisation failure. One instructor remained available. B’s new attempt then counted one and had to decline leaving. This is one observed victim and error point; neither is a promise about every run.</p>
<p>A result read inside a transaction that later aborts cannot justify a successful application action. Wait for the transaction’s outcome before reporting its work as accepted. <a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/">Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a> deepens the snapshot, locking-read and dependency behaviour, and compares another engine only where the difference changes an application decision.</p>

<h2 id="an-aborted-transaction-must-be-reconsidered-as-a-whole">An aborted transaction must be reconsidered as a whole</h2>
<p>A move can hold class 21 and wait for class 22 while another move holds 22 and waits for 21. Neither can finish. This cycle is a <strong>deadlock</strong>. In our native reversed-order test, PostgreSQL detected the cycle and aborted one transaction with <code>40P01</code>. Its earlier uncommitted changes did not survive rollback.</p>
<p>Acquiring shared resources in a consistent order removes that particular cycle. Keep the protected work bounded so other requests do not wait through an external call or an idle screen. Constraints, implicit locks and additional code paths can still introduce cycles, so deadlock handling remains necessary. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html#LOCKING-DEADLOCKS">locking documentation</a> describes its detection and the need to retry appropriate victims.</p>

<h3 id="retrying-one-statement-can-preserve-a-stale-decision">Retrying one statement can preserve a stale decision</h3>
<p>After the Serializable staffing abort, repeating “make Tariq unavailable” alone would retain the old conclusion that Mina can cover. A new transaction must repeat the reads and decision too. It now sees only Tariq available and refuses the change. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html">serialisation failure guidance</a> explicitly requires retrying the complete transaction, including the logic choosing its statements and values.</p>
<p>Separate three outcomes in the application. A business condition failed: no place was taken, and retrying immediately cannot create capacity. A known abort occurred: discard that attempt’s database decisions and reconsider the whole transaction under an appropriate retry policy. Or the outcome is unknown: find out what the operation did before deciding whether to repeat it. Limits, deadlines, backoff and driver error handling belong to <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#recover-according-to-what-the-application-knows">application recovery</a>.</p>

<h3 id="losing-the-reply-does-not-prove-rollback">Losing the reply does not prove rollback</h3>
<p>While COMMIT is in flight, a connection failure can look the same whether the database committed or did not commit. We tested the committed case with a local proxy that forwarded COMMIT, observed its completion at the server, then closed the connection without passing that reply to the client. The client reported connection loss. A separate observer found the committed reservation.</p>
<p>Before a commit request is sent, a disconnected ordinary session’s open transaction will not turn into a commit by itself. Once the request may have reached the server, the client’s missing reply is insufficient evidence of rollback. A received successful reply establishes a known commit under the configured durability contract. A failure after that does not undo it. Client libraries can report an <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/libpq-status.html">unknown transaction status</a> for a bad connection; that is uncertainty, not a business rejection.</p>
<p>Repeating the booking with a new request identity could consume another place. Retain a durable operation identity and a way to reconcile the result. A uniqueness rule helps keep that identity singular, but deciding what to return for a repeated request also requires checking that it refers to the same intended operation. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> carries that identity through the complete request and its recovery.</p>

<h2 id="commit-has-a-durability-boundary-and-a-system-boundary">Commit has a durability boundary and a system boundary</h2>
<p>A committed change need not already be in its final table page on storage. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-intro.html">write-ahead log</a> records enough information for recovery to reconstruct changes. With the ordinary local durability settings, commit waits for the required log flush; table pages can follow later. Memory alone cannot supply that crash guarantee.</p>
<p>The acknowledgement has meaning under a stated failure contract. Turning <code>synchronous_commit</code> off allows a reply before the local log is flushed; a crash can then lose recently acknowledged transactions. It differs from turning off safeguards such as <code>fsync</code>, which can risk corruption. Replication settings decide whether a commit waits for another server, and at which stage. A local durable log cannot save a destroyed disk, and an asynchronous replica may lack acknowledged changes at failover. These are choices to establish and test with <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>.</p>

<h3 id="a-database-rollback-cannot-undo-an-external-effect">A database rollback cannot undo an external effect</h3>
<p>Suppose the class charges a payment. Charging before database commit can leave a charge for a rolled-back reservation. Charging afterwards leaves a window in which the reservation exists but the payment call has not happened. Calling the provider while holding the transaction open does not make the provider’s charge part of the database rollback.</p>
<p>Figure 3 changes the failure point around one database commit. Compare what exists with what the application knows. The log path is a model of the documented durable-commit contract; the withheld reply is the failure window exercised separately in the native check.</p>
<p>The figure uses a pending instruction stored with the reservation. Select <em>After reply, worker stopped</em> to see why that instruction matters, then <em>Provider accepts, worker loses reply</em> to see the uncertainty it leaves.</p>
<CommitBoundary />
<p>One useful design records an instruction to perform the external work in the same transaction as the reservation. Commit then keeps both the booking and its pending work, or neither. A worker delivers that instruction later. This <strong>transactional outbox</strong> closes the gap between changing the database and recording intent; delivery can still be repeated, delayed or uncertain. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#make-work-beyond-the-transaction-recoverable">Recoverable external work</a> develops delivery, deduplication and reconciliation.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-argument-with-competing-sessions-and-failures">Check the argument with competing sessions and failures</h2>
<p>Use a disposable database and two independently connected clients. The setup below is deliberately smaller than the class schema in the design article: it isolates capacity, ownership and the separate staffing rule. The SQL article’s <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#start-with-a-database-you-can-inspect-and-reset">first section</a> explains creating and inspecting a practice database. Run this setup only there; it drops the exercise tables.</p>
<details><summary>Complete exercise setup and reset</summary><Sql code={setup} label="Reset the transaction exercise in a disposable PostgreSQL database" /></details>
<p>Record <code>SELECT version()</code>, the isolation level, commit settings, statement and lock timeouts. In each client use explicit <code>BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL …</code> so the run does not depend on an unseen connection default. Keep each pause visible: wait for a returned result or an observed lock wait before allowing the next step. Do not use guessed sleeps as evidence that the intended interleaving happened.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Controlled checks for the reservation argument"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Controlled order and expected evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Stale decision</td><td>Reset. A and B begin Read Committed and each read one. A writes zero, inserts R1 for owner 7 and commits; B writes zero, inserts R2 for owner 8 and commits. Expect two reservations and a failed invariant check.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Conditional contention</td><td>Reset. A conditionally decrements and pauses before commit. B attempts the same update. Confirm B waits, then let A insert and commit. Expect B to change zero rows; B inserts nothing and rolls back.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Initially absent identity</td><td>Reset. Both read no reservation for the same request identity. A inserts it; B tries to insert it too. After A commits, expect B’s insert to fail uniqueness. Roll B back. The pre-checks did not enforce the rule.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Known abort</td><td>Reset. Both begin Serializable and count two available staff. Each marks a different person unavailable. Commit both attempts. Expect at most one accepted departure; an aborted attempt must repeat the count. Do not assert which transaction must fail.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Unknown outcome</td><td>In a disposable client path, interrupt the commit response. Check the client’s knowledge separately from a new observer’s state. The recorded run withheld a completed COMMIT reply; it did not crash the server.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>After each reservation case, check both the response and the complete state. The equality query below must return <code>agrees = true</code> for each class in a valid committed state. A separate check should find no negative remaining count, duplicate owner or invalid reference. The deliberately broken case demonstrates why checking only the counter misses overselling.</p>
<Sql code={invariant} label="Check reservation ownership against the remaining-place counter" />
<p>The native run checked the displayed reservation statement, rollback, stale overwrite, conditional contention, concurrent uniqueness, visibility, staffing at Repeatable Read and Serializable, a reversed-order deadlock and the withheld reply. It used PostgreSQL 18.6 with <code>fsync</code>, <code>synchronous_commit</code> and <code>full_page_writes</code> on. No server crash, power failure, replica or payment provider was tested.</p>
<p>A passing run supports an argument; it cannot cover every schedule or writer. For our first implementation, the argument is that every successful reservation takes one unit through the guarded row update and records ownership in the same transaction; failed insertion rolls that unit back; the database arbitrates competing changes to that row; and every other writer preserves the counter–reservation equality. Challenge the last premise with a double cancellation, a capacity reduction and a move. If any path bypasses it, repair the protocol before relying on the test.</p>
<p>Recheck when the engine, isolation setting, query or writer set changes. Continue through <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a> for the request’s remaining obligations. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/locking/">Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> when the decision needs an explicit protocol, or <a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/">Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a> when its engine contract needs closer examination.</p>

